at line 194 and the XOR rule at line 190, so genuinely suspect wires are dropped from the set. This directly feeds the HIGH issue above, since a suspect set of the wrong size is what gets returned.\n\nSuggested fix: require *both* inputs to be bit 0.\n\n```ts\nconst isHalfAdder = fromInputs && gate.in1.slice(1) === '00' && gate.in2.slice(1) === '00';\n```\n\nThe equivalent code in `reconstruct.ts` is not affected — it uses `inputKey`, which sorts the two names, so it is correctly order-insensitive.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM — Worst case burns the entire 4s budget and returns a wrong answer\n\n**`/workspace/src/analyze.ts:19, 328, 336`**\n\nI built a 45-bit circuit (a correct adder plus two decoy gates `{XOR x20 y20 -> dec1}` / `{AND x20 y20 -> dec2}`, then 4 swaps). Reproducibly: **4010ms, returns `[\"dec1\"]`** — one wire. So the pathological case is not merely slow, it spends the whole budget and then returns a single wrong name via the unguarded line 336.\n\nThe decoys collide in `buildIndex`'s `byInputs` map (line 52: `byInputs.set(...)` with no collision handling, so the last gate with a given op+input-pair silently wins). That misdirects `scan`, `reconstructSwaps` returns null, and both fallbacks then flail for the full 4s.\n\nOn timing itself I could not push total runtime past 5s in any construction I tried (worst observed 4024ms with 822 gates), so the budget holds — but only because `TIME_BUDGET_MS` is 4000 with 1000ms of slack. The failure mode is answer quality, not timeout.\n\nSuggested fix: make `byInputs` a `Map<string, Gate[]>` so ambiguous lookups feed all candidates into the existing `resolve` shortlist rather than silently discarding one. That is where `resolve`'s candidate-list design already wants to be used.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM — `Circuit` does not detect a single-gate self-loop\n\n**`/workspace/src/circuit.ts:70`**\n\n```ts\nif (source >= 0 && source !== i) {\n```\n\nThe `source !== i` guard exists so a gate is not counted as its own predecessor, 