s:4`** (`/^[xy]\\d\\d$/`) and **`/workspace/src/circuit.ts:10-12`** (`padStart(2, '0')`)\n\nAnything above bit 99 is unrepresentable, and a wire named `x100` is not recognised as an input at all — `countInputBits` returns 0 and `findSwappedWires` returns `[]`. The spec caps input bits at 45, and I confirmed 9/10/11/45-bit adders all round-trip correctly (so the ≥10 zero-padding concern in the brief is a non-issue: `z10`, `z45` are all two digits and handled). Flagging only because the limit is implicit. Suggested fix: a comment at `XY_WIRE` noting the two-digit assumption, or relax to `/^[xy]\\d{2,}$/` with `padStart(2,'0')` still used for generation.\n\n---\n\n### LOW — `structuralSuspects`'s `bits >= 2` guard is unreachable dead logic\n\n**`/workspace/src/analyze.ts:180`**\n\n```ts\nif (bits >= 2 && gate.op !== 'OR') suspects.add(gate.out);\n```\n\n`findSwappedWires` returns early at line 319 when `bits === 0`, and for `bits === 1` the top z wire is driven by an AND (the half-adder carry), which is the case the guard is presumably protecting. But `structuralSuspects` is only ever reached after `reconstructSwaps` fails, and for `bits === 1` `scan` handles the circuit entirely at lines 154-157. The guard is defensible defensively but is not exercised; either add a `bits === 1` test that reaches it or drop it with a comment.\n\n---\n\n### LOW — `fanIn` rebuilds the producer map on every call\n\n**`/workspace/src/analyze.ts:235-237`**\n\n`fanIn` constructs a fresh `Map` over all gates each time, and it is called twice per `explore` node (lines 270-271), inside the recursive search. With ~220 gates and a deep search this is redundant O(gates) work per node. It never dominated in my measurements (the `Circuit` constructions at line 284 dominate), so this is a cleanliness point rather than a hot spot. Suggested fix: build the producer map once per `explore` invocation and pass it in, or hoist it into `searchRepair` and rebuild only when `current` changes.\n\n---\n\n### LOW — Unclear naming and a slightly misleading comment\n\n- **`/workspace/src/analyze.ts:321-322, 328`**: `started` is used only to derive two deadlines, and the half-budget split is expressed as `started + TIME_BUDGET_MS / 2` at the call site while the full deadline is a named `deadline` const. Naming the second one (`reconstructDeadline`) would make the intent at line 326's comment (\"the reconstruction only gets half the time budget\") self-evident.\n- **`/workspace/src/reconstruct.ts:245`**: `budget % CLOCK_INTERVAL === 0` works (`budget` decrements from 200000, so the check fires on a stride of 256), but it reads as if it depends on `MAX_NODES` being a multiple of `CLOCK_INTERVAL`, which it is not (200000 % 256 === 64). A comment or `(MAX_NODES - budget) % CLOCK_INTERVAL === 0` would remove the head-scratch.\n- **`/workspace/src/simulate.ts:50-51`**: comment says \"by an input or a duplicate gate\" — accurate, but see the MEDIUM item above; the comment describes behaviour that is arguably a defect rather than a design choice.\n\n---\n\n### Plan alignment\n\nBoth required exports are present and correctly typed against the spec's signatures (`/workspace/src/index.ts:1-3`). The performance constraint is met with wide margin on all realistic inputs (~150ms for the 45-bit suite vs a 5000ms budget), and the \"brute-force enumeration is not feasible\" guidance was respected — `perfectMatchings` is only ever applied to an 8-element set (105 pairings), never to the full gate list. No unjustified architectural deviations. The one plan-level gap is that the spec's guarantee of \"exactly 4 pairs / 8 wires\" is treated as an input assumption but never enforced on the *output*, which is the root of the HIGH issue.\n\n### Test coverage gaps\n\n`/workspace/tests/findSwappedWires.test.ts` covers 4/8/16/24/45-bit adders and the timing constraint well, but every assertion goes through `makeBroken`, which rejects functionally-invisible swaps — so the fallback paths in `analyze.ts`